#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065125} :activity/id #uuid "00e08faa-77cf-50f3-bce1-d22a42a5f138" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065447 :tutorial/content "Consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x)= 4x^2+x, \\qquad x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nThe domain restriction $x > 0$ means that $f$ is invertible on its entire domain. \n\nThe point $P$ with coordinates $(2,18)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x).$ This means that the point $P'$ with coordinates $(18,2)$ lies on the curve of the inverse function $y=f^{-1}(x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e16e532743be547c766e5a6a47bae8933a8dedb2b6d0fa0d60d47cc0b37ba04.png)\n\nHow do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at the point $P'?$\n\nFor this, we will use the fact that the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at $P'$ is the *reciprocal* of the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $P.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{{P}^{'}}=\\frac{1}{({\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{P})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at $P$ is straightforward. First, we differentiate $y=f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 8x + 1,\n$$\n\nand then plugging in $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_P = 8(2)+1 = 17.\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_{P'}= \\dfrac{1}{17}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this process using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nTo help remember this formula, note that it's okay to swap the derivative symbol (the prime) with the inverse symbol (the exponent of $-1$) as long as we take the reciprocal afterward." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1f70bcd-9b56-5fdd-ba18-58b625c05af0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9132 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1da740a0-e78c-558d-b559-528c35ca81d7" :step/math-academy-id 187680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063999 :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049352}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317279}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "16c7f862-0b61-56ca-b731-b881c09a79a7" :step/math-academy-id 187681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063998 :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409836}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"} :step/id #uuid "006b4fae-82f7-53a6-a492-7df130468acf" :step/math-academy-id 187684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065128}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065448 :tutorial/content "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and $f'(x)$ at some points. \n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n\nLet's use this data to find $(f^{-1})'(1).$ \n\nRecall that the formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(1) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(2)=1$, hence $f^{-1}(1)=2$, and also $f'(2)=12$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use the rule for calculating derivatives of inverse functions in conjunction with other rules of differentiation. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e542fea-8d0a-5c0c-ba69-3ec1685ecf92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9136 :tutorial/title "Calculating Derivatives Using Tables"} :step/id #uuid "2950a7d5-38ee-59bf-b277-9aeb378c6c62" :step/math-academy-id 187687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186